\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces (a)~Value rate $v(\theta )$ against signal speed at fixed signal variance $\mathrm  {Var}(\alpha )=1$ (normalized to $\theta =1$): faster signals are worth more under every friction, but transient impact makes the value grow only sub-linearly, $v\propto \theta ^{1-\beta }$, against $\theta ^2$ for pure risk. (Holding the innovation variance $\sigma ^2$ fixed instead confounds speed with amplitude, since $\mathrm  {Var}(\alpha )=\sigma ^2/2\theta $.) (b)~The causality gap for an OU signal and power-law kernel, $v_{\rm  ad}/v_{\rm  ant}=\sin (\pi \beta /2)$: adaptedness is nearly free for short-memory impact ($\beta \to 1$) and destroys the value for near-permanent impact ($\beta \to 0$).}}{7}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:value}{{1}{7}{(a)~Value rate $v(\theta )$ against signal speed at fixed signal variance $\mathrm {Var}(\alpha )=1$ (normalized to $\theta =1$): faster signals are worth more under every friction, but transient impact makes the value grow only sub-linearly, $v\propto \theta ^{1-\beta }$, against $\theta ^2$ for pure risk. (Holding the innovation variance $\sigma ^2$ fixed instead confounds speed with amplitude, since $\mathrm {Var}(\alpha )=\sigma ^2/2\theta $.) (b)~The causality gap for an OU signal and power-law kernel, $v_{\rm ad}/v_{\rm ant}=\sin (\pi \beta /2)$: adaptedness is nearly free for short-memory impact ($\beta \to 1$) and destroys the value for near-permanent impact ($\beta \to 0$)}{figure.1}{}}
