\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces (a)~Optimal positions on the same OU signal path ($\theta =\lambda =1$, $\mathrm  {Var}(\alpha )=1$, $\eta =0.5$ throughout), with the Markowitz target $\theta \alpha /\lambda $ (grey dashed) as reference. All three regimes damp positions relative to Markowitz; more persistent impact means a smaller optimal position (standard deviations: $0.375$, $0.328$, $0.269$ vs $0.87$ for Markowitz). The aim portfolio holds the largest of the three positions because temporary cost alone imposes no penalty on position size; adding exponential or power-law transient impact discourages holding large inventories. (b)~Rate step response: the analytical formula for the optimal trading rate per unit of a permanent step in $\alpha $, from the transfer functions of \S  \S  4--5, shown on a log lag axis. The aim portfolio (one EMA, decay rate $a=\sqrt  {\lambda /\eta }$) and the NV stationary solution (two EMAs with rates $b_1{<}a{<}b_2$) both decay exponentially; the slow NV component $b_1{<}a$ makes the NV response more persistent than the aim portfolio. The power-law regime decays algebraically, $\propto \tau ^{-(1-\beta )/2}$, the time-domain signature of fractional memory.}}{11}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:aim}{{5}{11}{(a)~Optimal positions on the same OU signal path ($\theta =\lambda =1$, $\mathrm {Var}(\alpha )=1$, $\eta =0.5$ throughout), with the Markowitz target $\theta \alpha /\lambda $ (grey dashed) as reference. All three regimes damp positions relative to Markowitz; more persistent impact means a smaller optimal position (standard deviations: $0.375$, $0.328$, $0.269$ vs $0.87$ for Markowitz). The aim portfolio holds the largest of the three positions because temporary cost alone imposes no penalty on position size; adding exponential or power-law transient impact discourages holding large inventories. (b)~Rate step response: the analytical formula for the optimal trading rate per unit of a permanent step in $\alpha $, from the transfer functions of \S \S 4--5, shown on a log lag axis. The aim portfolio (one EMA, decay rate $a=\sqrt {\lambda /\eta }$) and the NV stationary solution (two EMAs with rates $b_1{<}a{<}b_2$) both decay exponentially; the slow NV component $b_1{<}a$ makes the NV response more persistent than the aim portfolio. The power-law regime decays algebraically, $\propto \tau ^{-(1-\beta )/2}$, the time-domain signature of fractional memory}{figure.5}{}}
