\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Interpolation between the fractional-derivative and aim-portfolio limits, fixed power-law kernel ($\beta =0.5$, $\gamma =\lambda =\theta =1$), varying temporary-cost strength $\eta $. (a)~Adapted positions (stationary Szeg\H {o} filter applied to the same OU signal path as Figure~\ref {fig:aim}), with the Markowitz target $\theta \alpha /\lambda $ (grey dashed) for reference. All positions are damped relative to Markowitz and to each other: adding temporary cost ($\eta $) shrinks the optimal position because each unit of trading rate is penalized directly, forcing more conservative inventory targets. The two small-$\eta $ curves (green) are nearly indistinguishable because the power-law transient cost dominates both at the signal bandwidth; the divergence becomes pronounced only for $\eta \gtrsim 1$ where the temporary-cost crossover frequency $\xi _*=(\gamma c_\beta /\eta )^{2}$ falls below $\theta $. (b)~Rate step response $g_x(\tau )$ --- the optimal trading rate per unit of a permanent step in $\alpha $ --- computed via the Szeg\H {o} outer factor of the combined friction symbol $N(\omega )=\eta \omega ^2+\gamma c_\beta |\omega |^{1+\beta }+\lambda $, normalised at $\tau =0.3$, on a log lag axis. The dotted curve is the pure fractional limit ($\eta \to 0$, $\propto \tau ^{-(1-\beta )/2}$) and the dashed curve is the GP exponential limit ($\gamma \to 0$, $\propto e^{-at}$, $a=\sqrt  {\lambda /\eta }$). The five coloured curves sit between the two analytical limits and converge monotonically from the power-law tail (small $\eta $) to the exponential decay (large $\eta $) as temporary cost grows. }}{12}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:interp}{{6}{12}{Interpolation between the fractional-derivative and aim-portfolio limits, fixed power-law kernel ($\beta =0.5$, $\gamma =\lambda =\theta =1$), varying temporary-cost strength $\eta $. (a)~Adapted positions (stationary Szeg\H {o} filter applied to the same OU signal path as Figure~\ref {fig:aim}), with the Markowitz target $\theta \alpha /\lambda $ (grey dashed) for reference. All positions are damped relative to Markowitz and to each other: adding temporary cost ($\eta $) shrinks the optimal position because each unit of trading rate is penalized directly, forcing more conservative inventory targets. The two small-$\eta $ curves (green) are nearly indistinguishable because the power-law transient cost dominates both at the signal bandwidth; the divergence becomes pronounced only for $\eta \gtrsim 1$ where the temporary-cost crossover frequency $\xi _*=(\gamma c_\beta /\eta )^{2}$ falls below $\theta $. (b)~Rate step response $g_x(\tau )$ --- the optimal trading rate per unit of a permanent step in $\alpha $ --- computed via the Szeg\H {o} outer factor of the combined friction symbol $N(\omega )=\eta \omega ^2+\gamma c_\beta |\omega |^{1+\beta }+\lambda $, normalised at $\tau =0.3$, on a log lag axis. The dotted curve is the pure fractional limit ($\eta \to 0$, $\propto \tau ^{-(1-\beta )/2}$) and the dashed curve is the GP exponential limit ($\gamma \to 0$, $\propto e^{-at}$, $a=\sqrt {\lambda /\eta }$). The five coloured curves sit between the two analytical limits and converge monotonically from the power-law tail (small $\eta $) to the exponential decay (large $\eta $) as temporary cost grows}{figure.6}{}}
