\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Finite-horizon (Neuman--Vo\ss ) solution against the stationary filter; $\eta =0.5$, $\gamma =1$, $\kappa =2$, $\lambda =1$, $T=20$. (a)~The finite-horizon feedback gains $K(t)$ on inventory and impact state sit on the stationary constants $K_\infty $ (dashed) through the interior, departing only in the terminal boundary layer (backward Riccati). (b)~Optimal position for $\alpha _t=\sin \omega _0 t$: the finite-horizon rule (red) tracks the whole-line stationary filter (black) in the interior and deviates in the shaded boundary layers --- start-up from zero inventory and terminal unwind. The Riccati closed-loop poles equal the filter's moving-average rates $b_1,b_2$.}}{13}{figure.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:nv}{{7}{13}{Finite-horizon (Neuman--Vo\ss ) solution against the stationary filter; $\eta =0.5$, $\gamma =1$, $\kappa =2$, $\lambda =1$, $T=20$. (a)~The finite-horizon feedback gains $K(t)$ on inventory and impact state sit on the stationary constants $K_\infty $ (dashed) through the interior, departing only in the terminal boundary layer (backward Riccati). (b)~Optimal position for $\alpha _t=\sin \omega _0 t$: the finite-horizon rule (red) tracks the whole-line stationary filter (black) in the interior and deviates in the shaded boundary layers --- start-up from zero inventory and terminal unwind. The Riccati closed-loop poles equal the filter's moving-average rates $b_1,b_2$}{figure.7}{}}
